<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>If something is on fire right now</strong>
  Call/text the on-call number. Don't fill out a form, don't email. Speed beats process.
</div>

<div class="sop-meta">
  <div><strong>Trigger</strong> Phone call, text, or emergency form submission</div>
  <div><strong>Owner</strong> On-call rotation. Currently primary owner 24/7.</div>
  <div><strong>SLA</strong> &lt; 1 hr business hours / &lt; 4 hr after-hours, weekends, holidays</div>
  <div><strong>Output</strong> Issue resolved + post-incident note in CRM</div>
</div>

<h2>What counts as an emergency</h2>

<h3>Hard yes (drop everything)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Site is fully down or returning 5xx errors to visitors.</li>
  <li>Wrong contact info live: customers calling the wrong phone, going to the wrong address.</li>
  <li>Active promo with the wrong price.</li>
  <li>Suspicious form submissions (looks like an attack).</li>
  <li>Defaced page or content that shouldn't be there.</li>
  <li>Customer reporting a data leak or security concern.</li>
  <li>Pre-arranged seasonal launch (e.g. "Black Friday banner goes live tonight at midnight") didn't fire.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Hard no (route to maintenance)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>"I want to add a new service page."</li>
  <li>"Can we change the photo on the About page?"</li>
  <li>"My friend said the site looks dated."</li>
  <li>"I forgot my email password."</li>
  <li>Anything that doesn't have a customer-visible defect.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Edge cases (judgment call)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>"My competitor copied our copy" &rarr; not an emergency from our side; route to maintenance + advice on attribution.</li>
  <li>"Google ranking dropped" &rarr; investigate within a day; not a same-hour emergency.</li>
  <li>"Form is slow" &rarr; depends. If slow = broken (timeout, error), emergency. If slow = annoying, maintenance.</li>
  <li>"GBP got suspended" &rarr; not our infrastructure but I will help; treat as a high-priority routine task.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Response sequence</h2>

<h3>Minute 0-5: acknowledge</h3>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Pick up the phone or reply to the text/email within 5 minutes if at all possible.</li>
  <li>Get the basics: what is the customer-visible symptom, when did it start, what changed recently if anything.</li>
  <li>Set expectation: "I'm looking at it now. I'll have an update within 15 minutes."</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minute 5-15: diagnose</h3>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Open the site in a fresh browser. Reproduce the issue.</li>
  <li>Check Cloudflare dashboard: is the site up, is there a Cloudflare incident, is the DNS resolving correctly?</li>
  <li>Check UptimeRobot: was there a flap? When?</li>
  <li>Check the most recent deploy: did I ship something that could have caused this?</li>
  <li>Check the Cloudflare status feed for upstream incidents.</li>
  <li>If it's content (wrong info live): identify the file + the change needed.</li>
  <li>If it's infrastructure (down, errors): identify the layer (DNS, edge, origin, app).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minute 15-30: mitigate</h3>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>If a recent deploy broke it: roll back via Cloudflare Pages dashboard (one click). Live within 60 sec.</li>
  <li>If wrong content is live: fix the file, push, deploy. Same flow as routine update but on the clock.</li>
  <li>If DNS issue: check registrar records; revert to known-good if recently changed.</li>
  <li>If Cloudflare upstream incident: tell the client honestly + post a status note. Wait it out.</li>
  <li>If security concern: pull suspicious content offline, audit access logs, rotate any credentials that may be exposed.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Minute 30+: communicate</h3>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Update the client every 15-30 minutes during the incident even if status hasn't changed. Silence escalates anxiety.</li>
  <li>When resolved, send a clear "all clear" with what happened, what's fixed, when.</li>
  <li>Don't promise root cause analysis on the spot. Promise to send a writeup within 24 hours.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Within 24 hours: post-incident</h3>
<ul class="sop-checklist">
  <li>Write the incident notes in CRM: timeline, what we changed, what fixed it, how long it took.</li>
  <li>If it was caused by our deploy, write a "what I will do differently" note. Add to the QA checklist if applicable.</li>
  <li>Send the client a 1-paragraph postmortem. No corporate-speak. Honest.</li>
</ul>

