</div>

<h2>Common emergency patterns</h2>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Cloudflare regional incident</strong>
  Affects subset of visitors. Check status.cloudflare.com. Almost always resolves within 30-60 min. Notify client; wait it out. Don't try to "fix" what isn't ours.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Recent deploy broke a page</strong>
  Roll back first, diagnose second. Cloudflare Pages dashboard &rarr; previous deploy &rarr; "Rollback to this deployment". Live in 60 sec. THEN figure out what broke and ship a fix.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Client's domain expired</strong>
  Site goes dark within 24-48 hr of expiration. Most TLDs have a 30-day redemption window where the registrar can reactivate (often with a fee, ~$80). Help the client renew + redeem. Add stronger reminder to WHOIS-watch list.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Wrong info live (e.g. wrong phone)</strong>
  Fix immediately, even if it's small. The wrong-phone-number-live problem is one we should never have, but when it happens fix it in &lt; 5 minutes from notification.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout sop-callout--warn">
  <strong>Form submissions silently stopped</strong>
  Check Web3Forms dashboard for the access key + recent submissions. Check the recipient's spam folder. Test from incognito. If silent for &gt; 24 hr, treat as P1 emergency: real customers may be filling forms that nobody sees.
</div>

<div class="sop-callout">
  <strong>Client thinks something is broken but it's working</strong>
  Sometimes "the site is broken" means "I don't like this thing" or "I forgot how to do X". Stay patient. Ask: "Walk me through what you're seeing." Often the fix is education, not code.
</div>

<h2>What NOT to do</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Don't deploy a fix without verifying it works locally first. Two outages in a row are worse than one.</li>
  <li>Don't promise SLAs I can't meet. "Fixed in the next 5 minutes" sets up disappointment if it takes 20.</li>
  <li>Don't go silent during the incident. Update every 15-30 min even if status hasn't changed.</li>
  <li>Don't blame Cloudflare / the registrar / "something on Google's end" without verifying. Often the issue is closer to home.</li>
  <li>Don't treat one client's outage as a chance to upsell or scope-shift. Solve, write up, move on.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Exit criteria</h2>
<p>Issue resolved, client confirmed, post-incident writeup sent. Updates flow back to phase 09 maintenance steady-state.</p>
